- ws-close-payload-oob-read (lws-protocol.c:555,
  lwsjs_callback_protocol): the synthesized ws-close handling inside
  the CLIENT_RECEIVE branch reads in[0]/in[1] as the close code with
  no check that len >= 2. the genuine WS_PEER_INITIATED_CLOSE handler
  a few lines below (lws-protocol.c:625) correctly guards the same
  read with len >= 2. per rfc 6455 a close frame legally carries 0 or
  1 payload bytes, so this path reads past the frame payload whenever
  a peer sends a bare or 1-byte close frame.

    // requires a peer that sends a ws close frame with 0-1 bytes of
    // payload to a client connection, so CLIENT_RECEIVE synthesizes
    // the close-frame path instead of WS_PEER_INITIATED_CLOSE
    // handling it directly
    import { LWSContext } from 'lws';
    const ctx = new LWSContext({ port: -1 });
    ctx.clientConnect({
      address: 'example.com', port: 443, path: '/', ssl_connection: 1,
      protocol: 'chat',
    });

- raw-client-immediate-close-fires-error-not-close (lib/lws/protocols.js:485,
  RawProtocol.onClientConnectionError): closing a raw TCP client wsi
  (TCPSocket/TCPSocketStream) synchronously from within its own 'open'
  handler - i.e. right after LWS_CALLBACK_RAW_CONNECTED fires - never
  triggers onRawClose ('close'); instead onClientConnectionError
  ('error', with an empty message) fires once and that's the last
  event the wsi ever produces. confirmed with an evented TCPSocket:
  the 'open' listener logs then calls close(), an 'error' listener
  logs "error: " (empty message), and 'close' never fires, even
  waiting several seconds. looks like a native lws timing/state-race
  between the RAW_CONNECTED callback and the wsi's internal
  established-state transition, not a JS-level issue - a caller
  waiting on 'close' (or a *Stream's `closed` promise, whose
  StreamAdapter#error only rejects `opened` and never resolves
  `closed`, see protocols.js's StreamAdapter doc) hangs forever if it
  closes this early instead of waiting for 'open' to fully settle
  first.

    import { TCPSocket } from './lib/tcpsocket.js';
    const s = new TCPSocket('127.0.0.1', <any raw TCP listener port>);
    s.addEventListener('open', () => s.close());
    s.addEventListener('error', e => console.log('error:', JSON.stringify(e.message)));
    s.addEventListener('close', () => console.log('close')); // never fires

- spawnandwaitfor-leak-on-timeout (tests/unittests/subprocess-utils.js:91,
  spawnAndWaitFor): when a spawned client subprocess doesn't print its
  marker within timeoutMs, waitForLog() throws out of spawnAndWaitFor()
  before the caller ever reaches its own stopProcess(proc.pid) - the child
  qjsm process is never killed and spins at ~100% cpu forever (confirmed
  via ps: orphaned `qjsm /tmp/qjs-lws-test-*.js` processes still running
  minutes later). This was found via tests/unittests/test-server.js (since
  removed from the repo - every one of its subtests did `const log = await
  runClient(...); ctx.destroy();` with no try/finally, so the same timeout
  also skipped that subtest's own server ctx.destroy(), leaking the
  LWSContext's listening socket/epoll fd and keeping the whole qjsm process
  alive past its own "N out of M tests failed." summary line), but the bug
  is in spawnAndWaitFor() itself, which test-client.js also uses - not
  confirmed to reproduce there, just not ruled out. Not investigated
  further beyond confirming the leak mechanism.

- test-websocket-hs-rejected-on-sync-close (lws-protocol.c, lwsjs_callback_protocol;
  tests/unittests/test-websocket.js:158): "WebSocket.protocol() (server):
  the close event fires when the peer closes" fails deterministically
  (confirmed 3/3 runs) with "HS: Rejected at CLIENT_ESTABLISHED" delivered
  to onClientConnectionError instead of the close event firing. Root cause:
  the test's client calls wsi.close(1000, 'done') synchronously from
  onClientEstablished; lwsjs_callback_protocol() forces that dispatch's
  C-level return value to -1 whenever wsi.close() was called synchronously
  during it (s->closed, checked right after the JS_Call), and
  libwebsockets' client-ws.c treats a nonzero LWS_CALLBACK_CLIENT_ESTABLISHED
  return as a rejected handshake specifically (not a graceful close) -
  routed to CLIENT_CONNECTION_ERROR, which is mutually exclusive with ever
  firing CLIENT_CLOSED (see core-net/close.c's already_did_cce handling).
  Same mechanism documented at length in tests/unittests/test-ws.js's
  header comment, which works around it by deferring the close() call a
  microtask past onClientEstablished instead of calling it synchronously.
  Not clear whether this test ever reliably passed or was already flaky
  from the start; not otherwise investigated or fixed here since
  test-websocket.js isn't otherwise in scope for this session's changes.

    (run: env QUICKJS_MODULE_PATH="build/x86_64-linux-debug;." qjsm
    tests/unittests/test-websocket.js)

- async-dns-qtype-ignored-on-cache-fill (libwebsockets/lib/system/async-dns/async-dns.c,
  used via lws_async_dns_query() from the new ctx.resolve() binding in
  lws-context.c): querying the same hostname with qtype AAAA after (or
  instead of) qtype A returns both the A and AAAA records mixed together
  in a single result, rather than only the records matching the requested
  type. Confirmed against a real external name (example.com, via the
  sandbox's systemd-resolved stub at 127.0.0.53) - both { type: 'A' } and
  { type: 'AAAA' } queries in separate fresh LWSContexts came back with
  the same four addresses (two IPv4, two IPv6). Not narrowed further -
  could be the DNS cache keying by name only and not by qtype, or the
  query construction itself not restricting the wire request to one
  record type. Not in scope to fix here since it's inside the vendored
  libwebsockets submodule, not this project's own binding code.

    const ctx = new LWSContext({});
    console.log(await ctx.resolve('example.com', { type: 'AAAA' }));
    // -> includes IPv4 addresses too, not just AAAA/IPv6

- context-ref-counter-deferred-destroy-races-reuse (lib/lws/context.js,
  ContextRefCounter.release): found while fixing a since-resolved
  use-after-free in ContextRefCounter.release() itself - deferring
  `destroy()` to a microtask (that earlier fix) opened a narrow race: if a
  new `add()` lands on the same shared singleton context between the
  deferred destroy being scheduled and it actually running (e.g. one
  TCPSocketStream's last connection closes,
  and a fresh TCPSocketStream reuses the still-live shared context before
  the microtask fires), the deferred destroy still runs unconditionally
  and pulls the context out from under the new connection - it never
  opens, and anything awaiting it hangs forever. Reproduced with
  tests/unittests/test-tcpsocket.js's own back-to-back TCPSocketStream
  tests (a close in one test race against connect in the very next one).
  Fixed by rechecking the ref count inside the deferred callback itself,
  not just when scheduling it - if it's no longer zero (or markServer()
  was called in between), the deferred destroy is skipped.

    not practically reproducible as a short standalone snippet - needs
    two TCPSocketStream (or TCPSocket) instances sharing the class-static
    context, back to back, timed so the second's connect() lands in the
    same microtask window as the first's post-close deferred destroy;
    see tests/unittests/test-tcpsocket.js's "TCPSocketStream (client)"
    tests run consecutively for a real trigger

    (run from the qjs-lws project root: qjsm tests/unittests/test-tcpsocket.js -
    do not set QUICKJS_MODULE_PATH, see quickjs-module-path-crashes-module-load
    below, it causes a separate, unrelated crash)

