- repl-controlc-double-invokes-cleanup-handlers (/usr/local/lib/quickjs/
  repl.js:724-729, REPL#controlC; :259-264, REPL#exit): root cause of the
  user-reported "Ctrl-C doesn't stop ollama-repl, then a TypeError"
  symptom - confirmed directly with a standalone repro (below), not
  specific to ollama-repl or Gemini. On the *second* Ctrl-C in a row (the
  REPL's own "press again to quit" convention), `controlC()` runs every
  registered cleanup handler itself in a loop, then calls `this.exit(0)` -
  which runs the *same* `cleanupHandlers` Set through its own loop again,
  since nothing clears or dedupes it in between. Every registered cleanup
  handler therefore fires exactly twice on this path. For a handler that
  isn't idempotent - ollama-repl's own `repl.addCleanupHandler(() => {
  log.close(); client.destroy(); })` (examples/ollama-repl/repl.js:488)
  chief among them, since `OllamaClient#destroy()`/`GeminiClient#destroy()`
  (examples/ollama-repl/lib/{ollama,gemini}-client.js:~268) unconditionally
  call `this.#ctx.destroy()` and `this.#debugFile?.close()` with no guard
  against having already run - the second pass throws (a closed `std` file
  handle's second `close()` throws "invalid file handle", exactly the
  error the user saw). Worse: that throw happens *inside* `exit()`'s own
  cleanup-handler loop, which runs *before* `exit()`'s `std.exit(exitCode)`
  call - so the uncaught exception aborts `exit()` entirely and
  `std.exit()` is never reached. That's the actual "doesn't stop" part:
  the second Ctrl-C fully intends to quit and very nearly does, but a
  non-idempotent cleanup handler throwing during the redundant second pass
  silently prevents the process from ever actually exiting. (Separately,
  still true and worth fixing regardless: nothing aborts the in-flight
  chat()/chatStream() network call on SIGINT either - no AbortController/
  AbortSignal wired into GeminiClient#chat, OllamaClient#chat, or
  `lib/lws/protocols.js`'s HttpClientProtocol#connect() - so even a clean
  double-invocation wouldn't cancel a pending request, just tear down the
  context out from under it.) Root cause lives in `repl.js` from the
  vendored qjs-modules dependency (`/usr/local/lib/quickjs/repl.js`), not
  in this project - not fixed here. A local, cheap mitigation *is* in
  scope for this project though: making `OllamaClient#destroy()`/
  `GeminiClient#destroy()` idempotent (guard on a `#destroyed` flag, or
  null out `#debugFile` after closing it) would stop the second,
  redundant invocation from throwing and blocking `std.exit()`, without
  needing the upstream double-invocation itself fixed.

    import { REPL } from 'repl';
    const repl = new REPL('test', false);
    let calls = 0;
    repl.addCleanupHandler(() => {
      calls++;
      if (calls === 2) {
        const f = std.open('/tmp/marker.txt', 'w');
        f.close();
        f.close(); // second close on an already-closed handle -> throws
      }
    });
    repl.lastFun = repl.controlC; // simulate "second Ctrl-C in a row"
    repl.controlC();
    // throws "invalid file handle" from inside controlC() -> exit()'s own
    // cleanup loop, before exit()'s std.exit(exitCode) call ever runs;
    // `calls` is 2, not 1 - every cleanup handler fires twice on this path

